.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "d3-time-format"
.PP
This module provides a JavaScript implementation of the venerable \fRstrptime\fP and \fRstrftime\fP functions from the C standard library, and can be used to parse or format \fRdates\fP in a variety of locale-specific representations\&. To format a date, create a \fBformatter\fP from a specifier (a string with the desired format \fIdirectives\fP, indicated by \fR%\fP); then pass a date to the formatter, which returns a string\&. For example, to convert the current date to a human-readable string:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const formatTime = d3\&.timeFormat("%B %d, %Y");
formatTime(new Date); // "June 30, 2015"
.fi
.PP

.PP
Likewise, to convert a string back to a date, create a \fBparser\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const parseTime = d3\&.timeParse("%B %d, %Y");
parseTime("June 30, 2015"); // Tue Jun 30 2015 00:00:00 GMT\-0700 (PDT)
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can implement more elaborate conditional time formats, too\&. For example, here’s a \fRmulti-scale time format\fP using \fRtime intervals\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const formatMillisecond = d3\&.timeFormat("\&.%L"),
    formatSecond = d3\&.timeFormat(":%S"),
    formatMinute = d3\&.timeFormat("%I:%M"),
    formatHour = d3\&.timeFormat("%I %p"),
    formatDay = d3\&.timeFormat("%a %d"),
    formatWeek = d3\&.timeFormat("%b %d"),
    formatMonth = d3\&.timeFormat("%B"),
    formatYear = d3\&.timeFormat("%Y");

function multiFormat(date) {
  return (d3\&.timeSecond(date) < date ? formatMillisecond
      : d3\&.timeMinute(date) < date ? formatSecond
      : d3\&.timeHour(date) < date ? formatMinute
      : d3\&.timeDay(date) < date ? formatHour
      : d3\&.timeMonth(date) < date ? (d3\&.timeWeek(date) < date ? formatDay : formatWeek)
      : d3\&.timeYear(date) < date ? formatMonth
      : formatYear)(date);
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
This module is used by D3 \fRtime scales\fP to generate human-readable ticks\&.
.SH "Installing"
.PP
If you use npm, \fRnpm install d3-time-format\fP\&. You can also download the \fRlatest release on GitHub\fP\&. For vanilla HTML in modern browsers, import d3-time-format from Skypack:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script type="module">

import {timeFormat} from "https://cdn\&.skypack\&.dev/d3\-time\-format@4";

const format = timeFormat("%x");

</script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
For legacy environments, you can load d3-time-format’s UMD bundle from an npm-based CDN such as jsDelivr; a \fRd3\fP global is exported:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-array@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-time@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-time\-format@4"></script>
<script>

const format = d3\&.timeFormat("%x");

</script>

Locale files are published to npm and can be loaded using [d3\&.json](https://github\&.com/d3/d3\-fetch/blob/main/README\&.md#json)\&. For example, to set Russian as the default locale:
.fi
.PP
 js d3\&.json("https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3-time-format@3/locale/ru-RU\&.json")\&.then(locale => { d3\&.timeFormatDefaultLocale(locale);

.PP
const format = d3\&.timeFormat("%c");

.PP
console\&.log(format(new Date)); // понедельник, 5 декабря 2016 г\&. 10:31:59 }); 
.PP
.nf
